.TH ISOTONE-DAEMON 1 "2026-09-19" "Isotone 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
isotone-daemon \- the Isotone audio engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isotone-daemon
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isotone-daemon
owns a virtual PipeWire sink, runs Isotone's filters over everything played
into it, and plays the result into a hardware sink.
.PP
With no options it follows the default sink and moves each application's
playback into its own sink as the application appears, so the output the
desktop shows stays the hardware and choosing another one there moves the EQ
with it.
.PP
It is a user service, not a system one: it reads the session's PipeWire socket
and writes only the user's own configuration directory. Nothing it does needs
root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-sink " name"
Feed this sink, by
.IR node.name ,
instead of following the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-channels " n"
Channels of the virtual sink: 1, 2, 4, 6 or 8. The default is 2.
.TP
.B \-\-leave\-streams
Do not move applications' streams. Isotone's sink then has to be chosen by hand
to hear anything through it.
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " directory"
Read and write each sink's saved state here.
.SH USAGE
.PP
The package enables the service, and it starts at the next login. To control it
by hand:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl \-\-user start isotone\-daemon
systemctl \-\-user stop isotone\-daemon
systemctl \-\-user disable isotone\-daemon
.RE
.fi
.PP
To pin it to one sink rather than follow the default, override the command:
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl \-\-user edit isotone\-daemon
.RE
.fi
.PP
and set an empty
.B ExecStart=
followed by the one wanted; the empty line is how systemd is told to forget the
original rather than run both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR isotone (1),
.BR isotone-state (1)
